        We observe that the numerator's lowest-order term in $\rho_{xz}$ is $(a_1\hat{x}^2 + b_2 \hat{x} \hat{z}) \rho_{xz}$. Conversely, the denominator has a zeroth-order term  $\sqrt{a_1 \hat{x}^2 + a_3 \hat{z}^2 + 2b_2 \hat{x} \hat{z}}$.
        Because $f_k, g_{1,k}, g_{2,k}$ are continuous with respect to $\hat{x}, \hat{z}$, and under the condition $y \geq \delta_0$, we deduce that for any $\epsilon > 0$, there exists a positive constant $\delta_2$ such that for all $(x, y, z) \in B((0, \bar{y}, 0), \delta_2)$, the following inequality holds
        \begin{equation*}
            T_1(x,y,z) \leq \epsilon.
        \end{equation*}
        For $T_2(x,y,z)$, we have
        \begin{equation}
            \label{equation:page_18_1}
