Referring to Table \ref{Tab:results_linear}, the optimal test plan (lowest mean RMSE of 7,068) is a 2-point design: $S_1=0.2$  $(n_1 = 82)$, $S_2 = 0.9$ $(n_2 = 18)$.  When the number of stresses is not specified ($N=6^*$ design), the optimiser converges to 2-point design with similar test unit allocation to that of the $N=2$ design. Indeed, for each $N$ provided in Table \ref{Tab:results_linear}, the optimal high stress is $S_N=0.9$, and the optimal low stress is consistently $S_1\approx 0.2$. As per Figure \ref{Fig:data_linear}, $95\%$ of the lifetimes at the minimum stress ($S=0.1$) are right-censored, with much lower censoring ($5\%$) at $S=0.2$. Thus, for this linear setting, it is best to test more units at a lower stress where there is some (but not much) censoring, and fewer units at a much higher stress where there is more information per unit tested (i.e., at the higher stress, units fail much quicker and are more concentrated around the mean lifetime). Overall, it is clear that there are no statistical differences between the optimal test plans for different $N$ values when the RMSE values are considered in relation to the associated standard errors. In Figure \ref{Fig:Three_in_one_linear} we further illustrate these similarities by visualising each optimal test plan configuration.

