Figure \ref{Fig:Three_in_one_linear} shows how, for each $N$, the optimiser converges to $S_1\approx0.2$, $S_N=0.9$, with a higher proportion of test units allocated to the lower stress. For $N \in \{3,4,5,6\}$, we see that the optimiser has allocated a very small number of units to some other stresses. Given the complex and stochastic nature of the optimisation problem, it is highly likely that the optimiser has just not yet fully converged to a 2-point design within the given number of iterations (see note in Section \ref{sec:Optim_Results}). In any case, since these additional stresses are associated with such small numbers of test units ($\le 3$), and the different plans do not differ from each other statistically, all of this points to a 2-point design being optimal.\\

Next, we assess the performance of some practical variations of the optimal test plan. This provides practitioners with insights into some alternative test plans which, perhaps due to real experimental constraints, might be more convenient/favourable than the optimal test plan. (It should also be worth noting that such real-world physical constraints could also be embedded directly into our optimiser given its generality.) We consider the test plan: $S_1=0.2$, $S_2=0.9$, and assess various test unit allocations. \\\\

