\centering
\includegraphics[width=11cm, height=8.25cm]{test_plan_boxplot_linear}
\caption{Comparison of the optimal test plan against test plans with alternative test unit allocations. Test plan \#5 is the optimal test plan.}
\label{Fig:compare_linear}
\end{figure}

A comparison of variations of the optimal test plan is given in Figure \ref{Fig:compare_linear}. Here we compare the optimal test plan ($S_1=0.2$ ($n_1=82$), $S_2=0.9$ ($n_2=18$)) against test plans where the test units are rounded to multiples of five. When the test units are within $\pm5$ of the optimal allocation (test plans \#4 and \#6), the performance does not differ statistically from the optimal test plan (plan \#5). The average RMSEs of test plans \#3 and \#7 are higher than the optimal plan, but the performance  could still be considered reasonable in some real-world settings. When the test units deviate from the optimal plan by more than ten units (plans \#1, \#2, and \#8), the performance deteriorates considerably. This analysis shows that we can perturb the test plan slightly (to accommodate practical constraints) without any significant loss of information.\\

Overall, the optimisation results indicate that for Weibull lifetime data with a linear life-stress relationship, the optimal test plan configuration is to use two stress levels: the highest stress, and a low stress at which almost all failures are observed (i.e., there is very little censoring). In terms of test unit allocation, the optimal strategy is to allocate most units (approximately $80\% - 90\%$) to the lower stress.  These results are consistent with the literature. (See \cite{Meeker:1975}, \cite{Barton:1991} and \cite{Nelson:2005}, for example.)\\

In the following sections, we conduct a similar analysis using preliminary test data that assumes quadratic and power law trends. Once the estimated coefficients are obtained, we apply the optimisation procedure and assess the results as outlined in this section.

