It follows that 
\begin{align}
        \forall \bm{\mu}\in\M(\R,\R^D):\quad &C_1\norm{\norm{\bm{\mu}}_\M}_{A'}\leq\norm{\norm{\bm{\mu}}_\M}_{B'}\leq C_2\norm{\norm{\bm{\mu}}_\M}_{A'}\\
        &\Rightarrow C_1\norm{\bm{\mu}}_{A',\M}\leq\norm{\bm{\mu}}_{B',\M}\leq C_2\norm{\bm{\mu}}_{A',\M}.
\end{align}
The extreme-point characterization in Item 3 follows from \cite{unser2022convex}[Lemma 1 Item 3], which uses the monotone norm assumption. Then, the norm calculation is a  consequence of the fact that, if $\mu=\sum_{k=1}^{K}a_k\delta_{x_k}\in\M(\R)$ with $x_k\neq x_{k'}$ for $k\neq k'$, then $\norm{\mu}_\M=\sum_{k=1}^K\vert a_k\vert$.
