The total search time decreases monotonically with $D$. In the large $D$ limit, the total search time rapidly tends to an asymptotic value $\frac{2Le^{\beta}}{f^T}\left(\overline{\exp(-E_n)}+\langle t_{3D}\rangle k\right)$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:D}. This rapid convergence could explain why the sliding length of Cas9 is not very large when compared to that of TFs: increasing the sliding length by increasing $D$ might be evolutionary costly, without increasing performance very much. 

