\begin{abstract}
In 1950, Chien-Shiung Wu and her student published a coincidence experiment on entangled photon pairs 
that were created in  electron-positron annihilation. This experiment precisely verified the prediction of quantum electrodynamics. Additionally, it was also the first instance of a precisely controlled quantum entangled state of spatially separated particles,  although Wu did not know about this at the time.  In 1956,   Wu initiated  and led the so-called Wu experiment, which discovered parity nonconservation, becoming one of the greatest experiments of the 20th century. As Chen Ning Yang said, Wu's experiments were well known for their precision and accuracy. Experimental precision and accuracy manifested Wu's scientific spirit, which we  investigate here in some detail.  This paper is the translated  transcript of the speech the  author made at  the  International Symposium Commemorating 
the 110th Anniversary of the Birth of Chien-Shiung Wu, on May 31, 2022. The above abstract is the translation of the original abstract of  the speech.
 \end{abstract}

