Suppose now that society's preferences are given by the profile $P=(P_i)_{i\in N}\in\mathcal{P}(A_4)$ in \Cref{preference}, and that its choice is between the plurality and the Borda rules, so that $T=\{p,b\}$. Then, $p(P)=x$ and $b(P)=y$. Thus, the unique compatible profile $\boldsymbol{P}\in\mathcal{P}(T,P)$ is given by \Cref{preference1}. Then, $p(\boldsymbol{P})=b(\boldsymbol{P})=p$. Hence, the Borda rule is not \hyperref[b]{binary self-selective}.
