\section{Proposed algorithm}

For a single-user case, the phase adjustment is pretty simple. 

Assume a line-of-sight channel as known, and $\bm u_i$ is the first singular vector (or line-of-sight direction steering vector) of the single user channel matrix $\bm H_i$, here $i$ is the user index. Therefore, to maximize the received signal power, all subarrays should implement the beamforming with vector $\bm u_i$. Unfortunately, phase shifters can adjust only the corresponding phase as:

\begin{equation} \label{subarray_direction}
    \bm {\phi}_{m} = \angle \bm u_i^m, 
\end{equation}
where $\bm {\phi}_{m}=[\phi_{m1}\quad\phi_{m2}\quad...\quad\phi_{mL}]$ is the vector of phase states for the $m$-th subarray. Thus, for the $m$-th subarray, instead of the SVD-BF $\bm u_i^m$ we employ its approximation $e^ {\sqrt{-1}\angle \bm u_i^m}$ with a small loss in performance.

