We consider the problem of predicting future expectation values of a collection of quantum observables, given their noisy expectation values at past times. The measured observables, the initial state of the physical system and even the Hilbert space are unknown; we nonetheless assume a promise on the energy distribution of the state. Investigating to what extent extrapolation is possible in this framework, we discover highly problematic datasets that allow full predictability at any future time $\tau$, but only when past averages are known up to precision superexponential in $\tau$. We also find families of ``self-testing datasets", which allow practical predictability under reasonable noise levels and whose approximate realization singles out specific Hamiltonians, states and measurement operators. We identify ``aha! datasets", which drastically increase the predictability of the future statistics of an unrelated measurement, as well as ``fog banks": fairly simple datasets that exhibit complete unpredictability at some future time $\tau$, but full predictability for a later time $\tau’>\tau$. Finally, we prove that the extrapolation problem is efficiently solvable up to arbitrary precision through hierarchies of semidefinite programming relaxations.



