\section{Introduction}
Most experiments in quantum mechanics can be described as the preparation of a quantum system, followed by a demolition measurement thereof. This description encompasses all experiments in high-energy physics and quantum optics, and also most experiments in solid state physics. In many such experiments, one can further choose \emph{when} to carry out the measurement. This allows one to study the natural dynamics of the underlying quantum system, by exploring how the measurement statistics change with time. 

As opposed to classical physics, fast state changes in quantum mechanics are associated to highly energetic states \cite{speed_limits1}. This observation is the starting point of a vast corpus of literature on quantum speed limits, see, e.g., \cite{speed_limits1,speed_limits2,speed_limits3}, which relate the energy content of a quantum system with the speed with which it can change its state. Quantum states are, however, not directly observable: what one observes in the lab are measurement statistics. A complementary framework to study how measurement statistics behave as a function of time is, however, missing. In that framework, extrapolation of past system behavior would be a natural problem: given noisy measurement statistics of a quantum system at different times, can we predict the system's measurement statistics at some future time? 

In this paper, we explore this question under different constraints on the energy distribution of the underlying quantum state. More concretely, we study three cases: (a) the energy distribution of the state has support in $[0,E^+]$ (hard energy support constraint); (b) the energy distribution of the state has weight at most $\epsilon$ within the interval $[E^+,\infty)$ (soft energy support constraint); (c) the state has an average energy below a threshold $\bar{E}$ (average energy constraint).

We start our investigation by considering the quality of extrapolations one can make, given realistic experimental data. In this regard, we find families of time series with arbitrarily many datapoints for which extracting an accurate prediction requires much more accurate past measurement statistics. In fact, just making any \emph{non-trivial} claim about the statistical behavior of the system at time $\tau$ demands that noise in the data decrease as the inverse of a superexponential function of $\tau$. 

While such `problematic' datasets make extrapolation generally challenging, they do not tell the whole story: under hard energy support constraints, we also identify families of datasets consisting of very few datapoints that nonetheless allow one to make sensible predictions for some future time $\tau$, as long as the data noise is of order $1/\mbox{poly}(\tau)$. Such datasets are, moreover, \emph{self-testing}: in analogy with self-testing distributions in quantum nonlocality \cite{self_testing_review}, their approximate realization in the lab singles out, not just the system's whole timeline, but also the underlying initial state, Hamiltonian and measurement operators.


%We find that, in scenarios with a large number of datapoints within a given time interval, the noise of past measurement data required to make non-trivial extrapolations at time $\tau$ must in general decrease as the inverse of a superexponential function of $\tau$. Furthermore, for fixed $\tau$, the size of the extrapolation confidence intervals decreases infinitely slower than the data noise.

%These no-go results, discouraging at first glance, illustrate how difficult extrapolation problems can get, but they do not tell the whole story. Under hard energy support constraints, we also identify families of datasets consisting of very few datapoints that allow one to make sensible predictions for some future time $\tau$ as long as the data noise is of order $1/\mbox{poly}(\tau)$. Such datasets are, moreover, \emph{self-testing}: in analogy with self-testing distributions in quantum nonlocality \cite{self_testing_review}, the approximate realization of such quantum time series singles out, not just the system's whole timeline, but also the initial state, Hamiltonian and measurement operators involved.

In addition, we discover two unexpected extrapolation phenomena. 

The first one, which we dub the ``aha! effect", refers to a situation in which the time series generated by conducting measurement $1$ on a quantum system at specific times does not allow one to say anything at all about the measurement statistics at some future time $\tau$. However, if one also considers the corresponding dataset generated by a second, independent measurement $2$, then it is possible to predict the future outcome distribution of measurement $1$ with certainty. 

The second effect, which we call ``fog bank", describes an extrapolation scenario where the system's behavior at some future time $\tau$ is completely unpredictable, and yet it becomes fully predictable at some later time $\tau'>\tau$. 

We find that both effects can be observed in datasets generated by measuring closed, low-dimensional quantum systems.

We conclude our work by proving that the extrapolation problem is efficiently solvable for each of the considered energy constraints. The key to our result is a full characterization of the set of feasible time series under each listed energy constraint, through hierarchies of semidefinite programming (SDP) \cite{sdp} relaxations. 

Remarkably, we find that the set of feasible time series under the soft energy support constraint is not continuous in the measurement times. In fact, for some instances of the extrapolation problem, knowledge of the measurement times with infinite precision would allow an accurate prediction of the system's future behavior, while any imprecision whatsoever would lead to complete uncertainty. Nonetheless, through a straightforward adaptation of our techniques, we show how to make sound predictions in extrapolation scenarios where an adversary might have partial control over the actual measurement times.

The structure of this paper is as follows: in Section \ref{sec:framework}, we will introduce the framework of timed demolition measurements, which we will use to formulate all our subsequent results. Next, in Section \ref{sec:extrapolation}, we will introduce and motivate the extrapolation problem. We will investigate under which circumstances the extrapolation problem has a non-trivial solution in Section \ref{sec:non_trivial}, where we will also introduce the notion of self-testing datasets. In Section \ref{sec:phenomena} we will identify low-dimensional aha! datasets, as well as datasets exhibiting fog banks. We will next provide, in Section \ref{sec:charact}, SDP characterizations of the set of time series generated by quantum systems subject to each of the listed energy constraints. We present our final conclusions in Section \ref{sec:conclusions}.


Before we start, though, we quickly review past works with a similar flavor. As mentioned above, there exists a vast literature on quantum speed limits \cite{speed_limits1,speed_limits2,speed_limits3}, where the main goal is to relate the the energy content of a closed quantum system with the speed it can evolve into a perfectly distinguishable state. The focus is thus on states, not on the measurement statistics that they generate as they evolve. Energy considerations have also appeared in semi-device independent quantum protocols; in this regard, references \cite{Van_Himbeeck_2017,Van-Himbeeck_2019} consider the problem of bounding the statistics generated by measuring low-energy states of light under the Hamiltonian $H=\id-\proj{0}$. All measurements are assumed to take place at a given instant of time, and thus the Hamiltonian evolution of the states does not play any role in these analyses. The recent work of \cite{garner_2020,jones2024, Aloy_2024} deals with the problem of bounding the measurement statistics of quantum and supra-quantum systems subject to spatial rotations. This is mathematically equivalent to characterizing the time series generated by quantum systems subject to a Hamiltonian with spectrum $\{-J,-J+1,...,J\}$, where $J$ is an integer, or a half-integer. On this matter, one of our contributions is to show that such quantum datasets are SDP representable. To our knowledge, the general extrapolation problem, as defined in this paper, was first posed by one of us (and co-authors) in \cite{differential_paper}, as an example of a differential non-commutative polynomial optimization (DNPO) problem. For this class of problems, there exist complete hierarchies of SDP relaxations, which were used to solve an instance of the extrapolation problem under the hard energy support constraint. DNPO problems can just handle optimizations over bounded operators, so the techniques of \cite{differential_paper} cannot be adapted to deal with soft energy support or average energy constraints. In addition, the hierarchy of SDP relaxations proposed in \cite{differential_paper} is very computationally demanding and does not have bounds on the speed of convergence.


While writing up this manuscript, we became aware of the work of \cite{jones2025certifiedrandomnessquantumspeed}, where the possible measurement statistics of a closed quantum system at two different times are analytically characterized, assuming a bound on the system's energy variance. Their framework is essentially the same as ours, but restricted to just one measurement with two outcomes and only two datapoints. That work is focused on randomness certification, and the extrapolation problem is not considered.

\section{Timed demolition measurements}
\label{sec:framework}
Consider a closed quantum system, initially in state $\rho\in B(\H)$, for some Hilbert space $\H$, evolving through the action of a Hamiltonian $H$. Since we are free to choose the origin of energies, we can assume that $H\geq 0$. At time $t$, we conduct a demolition measurement labeled by $x\in\{1,...,X\}$, obtaining an outcome $a\in\{1,...,A\}$ with some probability $P(a|x,t)$. Measurement $M_x$ can be described by a Positive Operator Valued Measure (POVM), i.e., a tuple of positive semidefinite operators $(M_{a|x})_a\subset B(\H)^A$, with $\sum_aM_{a|x}=\id_\H$. With this notation, it holds that
\begin{equation}
P(a|x,t)=\tr(e^{-iHt}\rho e^{iHt}M_{a|x}),\forall a,x,t.
\end{equation}
For fixed $t$, we will call the vector of probabilities $(P(a|x,t))_{a,x}$ a \emph{datapoint}. The set of all datapoints will be denoted by ${\cal D}$. For finite ${\cal T}\subset \R$, any function $P:{\cal T}\to{\cal D}$, with $P(t):=(P(a|x,t))_{a,x}$, for all $t\in{\cal T}$, will be called a \emph{dataset} or a \emph{time series}. If we allow the domain of $P$ to include all $\R$, we will speak of a \emph{timeline}. Contrarily to datasets, timelines cannot be estimated through a finite number of experiments. Both for timelines and datasets, we dub the tuple $(\H,\rho,H,M)$ a \emph{realization}, where $M$ denotes the collection of measurements $M_x$. Figure~\ref{fig:setup} illustrates the setup for collecting datasets.
