We say that a timeline $P(t)$ \emph{fits} the noisy dataset $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ if the dataset $(P(t_j))_j$ complies with the constraints of the extrapolation problem. Let $\tau$ be a desired extrapolation time. If it holds that, for any $Q\in{\cal D}$, there exists a timeline $P(t)$ fitting $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ such that $P(a|x,\tau)=Q(a|x)$, then we say that the dataset $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ exhibits a \emph{Knightian uncertainty} at time $\tau$ \cite{uncertainty}. Under Knightian uncertainty, all possible extrapolation problems are trivial, i.e., the noisy dataset offers no help in making future predictions. The complete opposite of Knightian uncertainty is \emph{full certainty}: the situation that, for some $Q\in{\cal D}$, all timelines $P(t)$ fitting $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ satisfy $P(a|x,\tau)=Q(a|x)$.

Extrapolation problems naturally appear in many experimental scenarios. Firstly, experiments in quantum physics can be fragile and thus in some cases only run for short times. This can be due to difficulties in keeping the experimental  conditions stable (e.g.\ the setup may heat up when operated) or due to outside influences that we cannot fully shield our setup against (e.g.\ a cosmic ray hitting the experiment). In cases where these obstacles cannot be overcome, collecting data by running the experiment for different (short) times and extrapolating the results to the desired times offers a solution.
Secondly, simulating the time-evolution of large quantum systems (e.g.\ strongly correlated systems far from equilibrium) may be challenging due to their large number of degrees of freedom. In this case the black-box approach to extrapolation we propose here may be of interest, as it may allow us to make predictions about the values of certain observables after a long time, just from their (approximate) simulation for short timescales. 

%\textbf{We need a motivation here. Two ideas: 1) in statistical physics, sometimes one can approximately compute the evolution of the expectation values of an observable up to some error. Sometimes, the error suddenly increases, making further extrapolations impossible. In such circumstances, it would be useful to have some high-level extrapolation tools that used the generated series (with noise) to extend predictability to farther times. 2) Some experiments in quantum physics are interrupted (e.g., by cosmic rays). Can we say something about the experimental results that one could have gathered, had the experiment continued?}



\section{Conditions for accurate or non-trivial extrapolations}
\label{sec:non_trivial}

%The gist of Section \ref{sec:charact} is that, given sufficient computational resources, we can solve any extrapolation problem through hierarchies of SDP relaxations up to arbitrary precision. So now that we have tools to solve it, we can face the scariest of all questions: was the effort worth it? For all we know, it could be that all instances of the extrapolation problem are trivial.

In section \ref{sec:framework} we introduced different energy constraints to ensure that the extrapolation problem were not trivial. But, do they suffice? Is it possible to predict future measurement statistics solely from past data and energy assumptions?

To address this issue, consider the problem of extrapolating a \emph{time segment}, i.e., a map $P:[t_0,t_f]\to {\cal D}$. Under the assumption $P\in S(E^+)$, we have that the function
\begin{equation}
f(t):=\tr(e^{-iHt}\rho e^{iHt}\sum_{a,x}f_{a,x}M_{a|x})
\end{equation}
is analytic for all $t\in\C$. Given $P$ we can therefore expand $f(t)$ in a Taylor series and obtain $P(a|x,\tau)$ \emph{exactly}.

The above example is unrealistic, in that experimental data has noise and spans a finite set of datapoints. What happens in the (just demanding) regime $\infty>N\gg 1$, $1\gg\delta(x,j)> 0$? The problem of extrapolating noisy samples of an unknown analytic function $f(t)$ has been considered before in the computer science literature \cite{extr_analytic}. In \cite{extr_analytic2, extr_analytic3,extr_analytic}, it is shown that a least-squares fit by a polynomial of degree $D$ suffices to provide good bounds, in the afore-mentioned regime. The choice of $D$ solely depends on the maximum value of the function along a region in the complex plane that encompasses the times $t_1,...,t_N$: in our case, $D=D(E^+,\max_j|t_j|)$. The extrapolation method described in \cite{extr_analytic} certainly does not provide optimal extrapolations, but it shows that extrapolations are indeed possible, as long as the experimental dataset contains sufficiently many low-error datapoints.

The next interesting question is whether extrapolation problems are also non-trivial in realistic scenarios where both $N$ and $\delta$ take reasonable values. We find that the answer strongly depends on the experimentally observed datasets, which can be either problematic or unproblematic.


\subsection{A family of problematic datasets}
We next construct a quantum time series for which extrapolation is notoriously difficult, in that great precision on the datapoints is required to make precise, or just non-trivial, predictions. 

Consider the family of noisy datasets $\mathbf{O}(N, T,\delta):=(\tilde{P},\delta)$, with times $t_j=\frac{j}{N}T$, $j=1,...,N$, given by  
\begin{align}
&\tilde{P}(1|t_j)=\tilde{P}(2|t_j)=\frac{1}{2},\nonumber\\
&\delta(j)=\delta,\forall j.
\label{def_dataset_O}
\end{align}
Note that, since there is just one measurement setting, we have dropped the $x$ label. 

Our goal is to extrapolate the value $f(P(\tau)):=P(1|\tau)$. This defines the extrapolation problem $\mathbb{E}(N,T,\delta,\tau)$.

For any $N,\delta$, by symmetry the optimal prediction will be of the form $P(1|\tau)\in [\frac{1}{2}-\delta',\frac{1}{2}+\delta']$, for some $\delta'=\delta'(N, T,\delta,\tau)$. 

Under the hard energy support constraint, with vanishing noise $\delta\to 0$, and in the limit of infinitely many datapoints $N\to \infty$, the solution of $\mathbb{E}(N,0, T,\tau)$ is $P(1|\tau)=\frac{1}{2}$ by analytic continuity. Equivalently, 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\delta\to 0}\lim_{N\to\infty}\delta'=0.    
\end{equation}

A natural question is thus how small the extrapolation uncertainty $\delta'$ can be for finite values $\delta$ of the measurement error. The next proposition, proven in Appendix \ref{app:limits}, sheds some light in this respect.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop_nogo2}
Let $\tau>T$, and, under the hard energy constraint $\mbox{spec}(H)\subset \left[0,\frac{2}{T}\right]$, let the solution of the extrapolation problem $\mathbb{E}(N, T,\delta,\tau)$ be $[\frac{1}{2}-\delta',\frac{1}{2}+\delta']$. Then, for $\delta\ll 1$, it holds that
\begin{equation}
\delta'\gtrsim \frac{1}{2}\delta\left(\frac{\tau}{T}\right)^{\frac{\log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}{\log\log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}}.
\end{equation}
\end{prop}
Note that the proposition implies
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\delta\to 0}\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{\delta'}{\delta}=\infty.
\end{equation}
In other words: high precision in the extrapolation demands \emph{extremely} high precision on the measurement statistics.

This is bad news, at least if one expected to achieve very accurate predictions. Alternatively, one might be content with \emph{any} future prediction, as long as it is non-trivial. The next proposition, also proven in Appendix \ref{app:limits}, squanders this hope as well.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop_nogo1}
Assume the hard energy support constraint $\mbox{spec}(H)\subset [0,\frac{1}{T}]$. Then, for any $N\in\N$, the dataset $\mathbf{O}(N, T,\delta)$ has a Knightian uncertainty at $\tau>T$, unless $\delta\leq \frac{1}{g(\tau)}$, for some super-exponential function $g$, independent of $N$.
\end{prop}
The proposition thus precludes long-time extrapolations for the dataset $\mathbf{O}(N, T,\delta)$.

To make matters worse, due to the inclusion relations $S(E^+)\subset S(E^+,\epsilon),A(E^+)$, Propositions \ref{prop_nogo2}, \ref{prop_nogo1} also hold under the other two types of energy constraints.

\subsection{An unproblematic family of datasets}
The above two no-go results refer to worst-case performance: thus, they should not discourage us from attempting the extrapolation of experimental data. In fact, very simple datasets allow long-time extrapolations under reasonable experimental noise. This is the case, for instance, when the dataset is 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{D}_N(0|t_0)=1, \mathbf{D}_N(0|t_j)=0,j=1,...,N-1 ,
\end{equation}
with $t_j:=\frac{2\pi(N-1)j}{NE^+}$.
In Appendix \ref{app:ST}, we prove that just one timeline $P(t)\in S(E^+)$ satisfies $P(a|t_j)=\mathcal{D}_N(a|t_j)$, for all $j$. More specifically, the timeline with realization $(\C^N,\bar{H}^N,\proj{\bar{\psi}^N},\bar{M}^N)$, with
\begin{align}
&\bar{H}^N:=\frac{E^+}{N-1}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}k\proj{k},\ket{\bar{\psi}^N}:=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\ket{k},\nonumber\\
&\bar{M}^N_1:=\proj{\bar{\psi}^N},\bar{M}^N_2:=\id_N-\proj{\bar{\psi}^N}.
\label{reference_realization}
\end{align}
This means that, under no experimental noise, one can extrapolate $\mathbf{D}_N$ to arbitrary future times $\tau$. In addition, if the dataset is assumed to have some noise $\delta$, then non-trivial extrapolations for time $\tau$ are possible as long as $\delta\sim \frac{1}{\mbox{poly}(\tau)}$.

Furthermore, for any $N\in\N$, $\mathbf{D}_N$ is a \emph{self-testing} dataset, i.e., under the hard support constraint $\mbox{spec}(H)\subset [0,E^+]$, it can only be generated through the realization (\ref{reference_realization}), modulo isometries. More precisely, in Appendix \ref{app:ST} we prove:
\begin{prop}
\label{prop_self_test}
Let $P\in S(E^+)$, with realization $(\H,\rho,H,M)$, satisfy
\begin{equation} \label{eq:approx_data}
\sum_a|P(a|t_j)-\mathbf{D}_N(a|t_j)|\leq \delta,
\end{equation}
for $j=0,...,N-1$. Then, there exists an isometry $V$ such that
\begin{align}
&VM_ae^{-iHt}\rho e^{iHt}V^\dagger\nonumber\\
&=\bar{M}^N_ae^{-i\bar{H}^Nt}\proj{\bar{\psi}^N} e^{i\bar{H}^Nt}\otimes \gamma_{\mbox{junk}}\nonumber\\
&+O\left(\delta^{1/64}\right)+O\left(\delta^{1/8}t\right),
\label{eq_prop_self_test}
\end{align}
for $a=1,2$ and some normalized quantum state $\gamma_{\mbox{junk}}$.
\end{prop}

Both results rely on a technical lemma that will be of use later.
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma_density}
Let $N\in\N$, let $\ket{\psi}$ be a normalized state and let $\sigma(E)dE$ be its energy density with respect to some Hamiltonian $H$. Suppose that the hard support constraint holds, i.e., $\mbox{supp}(\sigma(E))\in[0,E^+]$ and that
\begin{equation}
\bra{\psi}e^{iH(t_0-t_k)}\ket{\psi}=0, k=1,...,N-1,
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
t_k:=\frac{2\pi (N-1)k}{N E^+}.
\end{equation}
Then, 
\begin{equation}   \operatorname{supp}(\sigma{(E)}) \subseteq \bigcup_{k=0}^{N-1} \frac{E^+k}{N-1}.
\end{equation}
%\begin{equation}
%\sigma(E)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\delta \left(E-\frac{E^+k}{N-1}\right).
%\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
We refer to Appendix~\ref{app:ST} for an error-tolerant formulation of the lemma and its proof.


