In the following lines, we show that these two phenomena, far from being hypothetical, can already be noticed in experiments with low-dimensional quantum systems. We will consider noise-free datasets $(\tilde{P},\delta)$, with $\delta(x,j)=0$ for all $x,j$. We note, however, that the two coming examples are \emph{noise-robust}: for $\delta(x,j)=\delta'>0$ sufficiently small, in the aha! dataset example the noisy dataset $(\tilde{P}_1,\delta)$ would still exhibit a Knightian uncertainty at $\tau$, while the composite dataset $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ would allow predicting $P_1(a|1,\tau)$ with \emph{almost} full certainty. Similarly, replacing $(\tilde{P},0)$ with $(\tilde{P},\delta)$ in the fog bank example, there would still be a Knightian uncertainty at $\tau_1$ and \emph{almost} full predictability at $\tau_2$. Both aha! datasets and fog banks are therefore experimentally observable. Whether fog banks and aha! datasets with full certainty exist in scenarios with noisy measurement data is an interesting question for future research.



\subsection{Two instances of aha! datasets}
In Appendix \ref{app:aha_2} we show that the self-testing dataset $\mathbf{D}_2$ is an aha! dataset for some other, two-point dataset. In this section, we use three-level quantum systems to engineer a more interesting example: a pair of datasets $\mathbf{A}_1$, $\mathbf{A}_2$ such that each is an aha! dataset for the other.

Define the measurement times $t_k:=\frac{4\pi k}{3E^+}$, for $k=0,1,2$, and consider the following datasets:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{A}_1&:=\{\tilde{p}(0|t_0)=1,\tilde{p}\left(0|t_1\right)=\frac{1}{3},\tilde{p}\left(0|t_2\right)=0\},\\
    \mathbf{A}_2&:=\{\tilde{p}(0|t_0)=1,\tilde{p}\left(0|t_1\right)=0,\tilde{p}\left(0|t_2\right)=1\}.
\end{align}

They represent the time series of two different measurements on the same physical system. As such, they can be integrated into a joint dataset $\mathbf{A}$:
\begin{align}
&\mathbf{A}(\bullet|x=1,\bullet):=\mathbf{A}_1(\bullet|\bullet),\nonumber\\
&\mathbf{A}(\bullet|x=2,\bullet):=\mathbf{A}_2(\bullet|\bullet).
\end{align}

There exists a simple, three-dimensional timeline $P^+$ that fits $\mathbf{A}$, with realization $(\C^3,\ket{\psi^+},H^+,M^+)$, where 
\begin{align}
&\ket{\psi^+}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}(\ket{1}+\ket{2}+\ket{3}),\nonumber\\
&H^+=\frac{E^+}{2}\proj{2}+E^+\proj{3},\nonumber\\
&M^+_{0|1}=\proj{\psi^+}+\frac{1}{3}\proj{\psi^+(t_1)},\nonumber\\
&M^+_{0|2}=\id-\proj{\psi^+(t_1)}.
\end{align}
Thus, $\mathbf{A}\in S(E^+)$. For $\tau=\frac{4\pi}{E^+}$, the timeline $P^+$ predicts $P^+(0|1,\tau)=P^+(0|2,\tau)=1$.

Next we define the timelines $P^{-,1}$, $P^{-,2}$, with realization $(\C^4,\ket{\psi^{(-,x)}},H^{(-,x)},M^{(-,x)})$, where
\begin{align}
&\ket{\psi^{-,1}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\ket{1}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\ket{2}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\ket{3}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\ket{4},\nonumber\\
&\ket{\psi^{-,2}}=\frac{1}{2}(\ket{1}+\ket{2}+\ket{3}+\ket{4}),\nonumber\\
&H^{(-,1)} =\frac{E^+}{4}\proj{2}+\frac{E^+}{2}\proj{3}+\frac{3E^+}{4}\proj{4},\nonumber\\
&H^{(-,2)} =\frac{E^+}{2}\proj{2}+\frac{3E^+}{4}\proj{3}+E^+\proj{4},\nonumber\\
&M^{(-,1)}_0=\ket{\psi^{(-,1)}}\bra{\psi^{(-,1)}},\nonumber\\
&M^{(-,2)}_0=\proj{\psi^{(-,2)}}+\proj{\alpha},
\end{align}
with
\begin{equation}
    \ket{\alpha}:= \frac{1}{2}\ket{1}-\frac{1}{2}\ket{2}+\frac{1}{2}\ket{3}-\frac{1}{2}\ket{4}.
\end{equation}

Then, one can verify that, for $x=1,2$, $P^{(-,x)}(t)$ fits the single-measurement dataset $\mathbf{A}_x$. Moreover, the timelines predict $P^{(-,x)}(0|\tau)=0$. 

In summary, for each single-measurement dataset $\mathbf{A}_x$, we have two fitting timelines: one that predicts $P(0|\tau)=1$ and another one predicting $P(0|\tau)=0$. Since any convex combination of the two defines a fitting dataset, we conclude that, on their own, both datasets $\mathbf{A}_1$, $\mathbf{A}_2$ exhibit Knightian uncertainty at $\tau$.

