\subsection{An instance of a fog bank}
Consider an experimental scenario with a single measurement setting and three outcomes $a=0,1,2$. For times $t_0,t_2,t_3$ (note that $t_1$ is missing), with $t_k=\frac{3k\pi}{2E^+}$, we define the dataset
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}:=\{\tilde{P}(0|t_0)=\tilde{P}(1|t_2)=\tilde{P}(2|t_3)=1\}.
\end{equation}
A simple family of $4$-
dimensional models fits the dataset, namely:
\begin{align}
&\rho=\proj{\bar{\psi}^4},H=\bar{H}^4, \nonumber\\
&M_0=\proj{\bar{\psi}^4}+q(0)\proj{\bar{\psi}^4(t_1)},\nonumber\\
&M_1=\proj{\bar{\psi}^4(t_2)}+q(1)\proj{\bar{\psi}^4(t_1)}, \nonumber\\
&M_2=\proj{\bar{\psi}^4(t_3)}+q(2)\proj{\bar{\psi}^4(t_1)},
\end{align}
where $q$ is an arbitrary distribution. At $\tau_1:=t_1+\frac{6\pi}{E^+}$, each timeline $P$ with the reealizatio above predicts the datapoint $P(a|\tau_1)=q(a)$. Therefore, there is Knightian uncertainty at $\tau_1$.

Let $(\H,\psi,H,M)$ be the pure state realization of any timeline $P(t)$ fitting $\mathbf{F}$. Then $\tilde{P}(0|t_0)=1$, $\tilde{P}(0|t_2)=0$ implies that the state $\ket{\psi(t_2)}$ is orthogonal to $\ket{\psi(t_0)}$. Similarly, $\tilde{P}(0|t_0)=1$, $\tilde{P}(0|t_3)=0$ implies that the state $\ket{\psi(t_3)}$ is orthogonal to $\ket{\psi(t_0)}$. 

The state $\ket{\psi(t_1)}$ is similarly orthogonal to $\ket{\psi(t_0)}$, since
\begin{align}
&\braket{\psi(t_0)}{\psi(t_1)}=\bra{\psi}e^{-iHt_1}\ket{\psi}\nonumber\\
&=\bra{\psi}e^{-iH(t_3-t_2)}\ket{\psi}=\braket{\psi(t_2)}{\psi(t_3)}=0,
\end{align}
where the last relation follows from $\tilde{P}(1|t_2)=1,\tilde{P}(1|t_3)=0$.

We can then apply Lemma \ref{lemma_density} and conclude that the energy density of $\ket{\psi}$ has support in $\left\{\frac{kE^+}{3}:k=0,1,2,3\right\}$. Consequently, $\ket{\psi(t)}=\ket{\psi\left(t+\frac{6\pi}{E^+}\right)}$, and so, for $\tau_2:=t_2+\frac{6\pi}{E^+}>\tau_1$, we have that
\begin{equation}
P(1|\tau_2)= P(1|t_2)=1.
\end{equation}
Hence, there is full certainty at $\tau_2>\tau_1$, which implies that we have a fog bank at $\tau=\tau_1$.

