\section{Resolution of the extrapolation problem}
\label{sec:charact}

In this section we explain how to characterize the sets $S(E^+),S(E^+;\epsilon), A(\bar{E})$ through complete sequences of semidefinite programming (SDP) relaxations \cite{sdp}. Namely, for $T\in\{S(E^+),S(E^+;\epsilon), A(\bar{E})\}$ we will show that there exists a sequence of SDP-representable sets $(T_k)_k$ such that $T\subset T_k$, for all $k$ and $\lim_{k\to\infty}T_k=\overline{T}$. Moreover, for any sequence of natural numbers $(m^j)_j$ such that $m^j$ is a multiple of $m^{j-1}$, it will hold that
\begin{equation}
T_{m^1}\supset T_{m^2}\supset...\supset T,
\end{equation}
namely, the sequence of sets $(T_{m^j})_j$ will form an SDP \emph{hierarchy}.

Given sufficient computational power, such SDP sequences of relaxations will allow us to solve the extrapolation problem for all practical purposes. Indeed, call $\mu^\pm_m$ the solutions of the semidefinite program that results when one replaces $T$ by $T_m$ in (\ref{extrapol_optim}). Since $T\subset T_m$, we have that $f(P(\tau))\in [\mu^-_m,\mu^+_m]$ for any $P\in T$ compatible with the experimental data $(\tilde{P},\delta)$. In other words, each SDP relaxation $T_m$ allows us to make a testable prediction for time $\tau$. In addition, since $\lim_{m\to\infty} T_m=\overline{T}$, we have that $\lim_{m\to\infty}[\mu^-_m,\mu^+_m]$ tends to the optimal extrapolation interval. More specifically:
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma_T_m_approx}
Let $(P,\delta,f)$ be an extrapolation problem, and let $T_m\supset T$ be  a relaxation of $T$ such that, for any $Q\in T_m$, there exists $\tilde{Q}\in T$ with $\|Q-\tilde{Q}\|\leq \epsilon_m$. Further assume that there exists $P^0\in T$ and $r>0$ with
\begin{equation}
\delta(x,j)-\sum_a|P^0(a|x,t_j)-P(a|x,t_j)|\geq r,\forall x,j.
\end{equation}
Then it holds that
\begin{equation}
|\mu_m^{\pm}-\mu^{\pm}|\leq \epsilon_m\left(\frac{|\mu^{\pm}-f(P^0(\tau))|}{r}+\sum_{x}\max_{a}|f(a|x)|\right).
\end{equation}


\end{lemma}
For a proof of the lemma, see Appendix \ref{app:T_m_approx}.



\subsection{Hard energy constraints}

Before characterizing $S(E^+)$, we will solve a simpler, related problem.

Given ${\cal E}:=\{E_j\}_{j=1}^m\subset \R$, consider the set $S({\cal E})$ of datasets with realizations $(\H,\rho,H,M)$ such that $\mbox{spec}(H)\subset {\cal E}$. We next show that the membership of a dataset in $S({\cal E})$ can be decided through a single SDP. This solves, in particular, the problem of characterizing the set ${\cal Q}_{J,A}$ of spin-bounded quantum rotation boxes \cite{Aloy_2024}.

For an arbitrary dataset $P\in S({\cal E})$, let $(\H,\ket{\psi},H,M)$ be a pure-state realization of $P$. Then, $\ket{\psi}=\sum_{j=1}^mc_j\ket{E_j}$, for some $\{c_j\}_j\subset \C$ and normalized vectors $\{\ket{E_j}\}_j$ such that $H\ket{E_j}=E_j\ket{E_j}$, for all $j$. Define $\hat{\H}:=\mbox{span}\{\ket{E_j}\}_j$. Then, $\ket{\psi(t)}:=e^{-iHt}\ket{\psi}\in\hat{\H}$, for all $t$. It follows that the realization $(\hat{\H},\proj{\psi},H_{|\tilde{\H}},M_{|\tilde{\H}})$ generates the same timeline $P(t)$ as the original one. Since $\mbox{dim}(\hat{\H})\leq m$, we can embed everything into $\C^m$, via the correspondence $\ket{E_j}\to\ket{j}$, where $\{\ket{j}\}_{j=1}^m$ denote the elements of the computational basis in $\C^m$. We arrive at the equivalent realization $(\C^m,\proj{\hat{\psi}}, \hat{H},\hat{M})$, with $\ket{\hat{\psi}}=\sum_jc_j\ket{j}$, $\hat{H}=\sum_jE_j\proj{j}$.

Next, note that $\ket{\hat{\psi}(t)}=\Lambda\ket{\Phi(t)}$, with
\begin{equation}
\Lambda:=\mbox{diag}(c_1,...,c_m),\ket{\Phi(t)}:=\sum_{j=1}^me^{-iE_jt}\ket{j}.
\end{equation}
We thus have that
\begin{equation}
P(a|x,t)=\bra{\Phi(t)} \tilde{M}_{a|x}\ket{\Phi(t)},
\label{prob_via_M_tilde}
\end{equation}
with $\tilde{M}_{a|x}:=\Lambda^\dagger M_{a|x}\Lambda$. It is straightforward to see that $\tilde{M}_{a|x}\geq 0$, for all $a,x$. In addition, $\sum_a\tilde{M}_{a|x}=\mbox{diag}(|c_1|^2,...,|c_m|^2)$. The trace of this matrix is the norm of $\ket{\hat{\psi}}$, i.e., $1$.

To recap: we have just proven that, for any timeline $P\in S(\{E_j\}_j)$, there exist $m\times m$ positive semidefinite complex matrices $\{\tilde{M}_{a|x}\}_{a,x}$ such that $\sum_{a}\tilde{M}_{a|x}$ equals a trace-$1$ diagonal matrix, independent of $x$, and such that eq. (\ref{prob_via_M_tilde}) holds for all $x,a$. 

Conversely, it is easy to see that, if $P$ can be expressed as (\ref{prob_via_M_tilde}), for some $\tilde{M}_{a|x}$ satisfying the positivity and normalization constraints above, then $P\in S({\cal E})$. Indeed, it suffices to define $\Lambda:=\sqrt{\sum_{a}\tilde{M}_{a|x}}$ and note that $(\C^m,\proj{\psi},\sum_{j}E_j\proj{j},M)$, with $\ket{\psi}=\Lambda\ket{\Phi(0)}$, $M_{a|x}=\Lambda^{-1}\tilde{M}_{a|x}\Lambda^{-1}$ is a realization of $P$.

The set of datasets $S({\cal E})$ is therefore SDP representable for finite ${\cal E}$. 

To characterize the more complex set $S(E^+)$, we introduce the discretization function
\begin{equation}
\varphi_m(E):=\frac{E^+}{m}\mbox{round}\left(\frac{mE}{E^+}\right).
\end{equation}
This function maps any energy $E\in [0,E^+]$ to the closest value in the finite set $\left\{\frac{kE^+}{m}:k=0,...,m\right\}$; consequently, $|E-\varphi_m(E)|\leq \frac{E^+}{2m}$. Now, let $(\H,\proj{\psi},H,M)$ be a realization for $P$, with $\mbox{spec}(H)\subset [0,E^+]$. Consider the state trajectory $\ket{\tilde{\psi}(t)}:=e^{-i\tilde{H}t}\ket{\psi}$, with $\tilde{H}:=\varphi_m(H)$. For $m\gg 1$, it holds that $\ket{\psi(t)}\approx \ket{\tilde{\psi}(t)}$. Indeed, let $m\geq \frac{E^+|t|}{\pi}$ and call $\sigma(E)dE$ the energy distribution of $\ket{\psi}$ (for the Hamiltonian $H$). Then we have that
\begin{align}
&|\braket{\psi(t)}{\tilde{\psi}(t)}|=\left|\int_{0}^{E^+}dE\sigma(E)e^{i(E-\varphi_m(E))t}\right|\nonumber\\
&\geq \int_{0}^{E^+}dE\sigma(E)\cos\left((E-\varphi_m(E))t\right)\geq \cos\left(\frac{E^+t}{2m}\right).
\end{align}
Call $\tilde{P}$ the timeline with realization $(\H,\proj{\psi},\tilde{H},M)$ (notice that $\tilde{P}\in S(E^+)$). For any $x$, the above relation implies that 
\begin{align}
&\sum_a \left|P(a|x,t)- \tilde{P}(a|x,t)\right|\leq \|\proj{\psi(t)}-\proj{\tilde{\psi}(t)}\|_1\nonumber\\
&\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+|t|}{2m}\right).
\label{diff_Ps}
\end{align}
This motivates the following definition:
\begin{defin}
$S_m(E^+)$ is the set of datasets for which there exists a distribution $\tilde{P}\in S(\{\frac{k}{m}E^+:k=0,...,m\})$ satisfying eq. (\ref{diff_Ps}), for $t=t_1,...,t_N$ and for all $x$.    
\end{defin}
Now we can easily formulate what we have just proven.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop_char_S}
Let $m\in\N,$ $m\geq \frac{E^+\max_j|t_j|}{\pi}$. Then $S(E^+)\subset S_m(E^+)$. Moreover, for any $P\in S_m(E^+)$ there exists $\tilde{P}\in S(E^+)$ such that eq. (\ref{diff_Ps}) holds for $t=t_1,...,t_N$.

\end{prop}
Clearly, $S_m(E^+)$ is SDP representable, and thus deciding membership of a dataset in $S_m(E^+)$ or conducting linear optimizations over this set can be cast as an SDP. Moreover, eq. (\ref{diff_Ps}) implies that $\lim_{m\to \infty}S_m(E^+)=\overline{S(E^+)}$.



\subsection{Average energy constraint}
\label{sec:average}
We next show how to characterize the set of datasets $A(\bar{E})$ by means of a hierarchy of SDP relaxations.

Once more, the strategy is to exploit discretization. Let $m\in\N$, and consider the discretization function $\underline{\varphi}_m:\R_{\geq 0}\times \R^+\to \R^+$ defined by:
\begin{equation}
\underline{\varphi}_m(E,E^+):=\left\lfloor\frac{E}{\Delta_m}\right\rfloor\Delta_m,
\label{def_floor_disc}
\end{equation}
with $\Delta_m:=\frac{E^+}{m}$.
Then we have that
\begin{equation}
0\leq E-\underline{\varphi}_m(E,E^+)<\Delta_m,\forall E.
\label{discre_phi}
\end{equation}

Consider now a dataset $P\in A(\bar{E})$, with pure-state realization $(\H, H, M,\psi)$. For $m\in\N$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
m\geq \max_j\frac{E^+|t_j|}{\pi},
\label{condi_times}
\end{equation}
define $\tilde{H}=\underline{\varphi}_m(H,E^+)$, for some $E^+>\bar{E}$. Invoking equations (\ref{discre_phi}), (\ref{condi_times}) and following the derivation of eq. (\ref{diff_Ps}), we arrive at the inequality
\begin{equation}
\sum_a|P(a|x,t_j)-\tilde{P}(a|x,t_j)|\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+|t_j|}{m}\right),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\tilde{P}(a|x,t):=\bra{\psi}e^{i\tilde{H}t}M_{a|x} e^{i\tilde{H}t}\ket{\psi}.
\end{equation}

Since the Hamiltonian $\tilde{H}$ satisfies 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{spec}(\tilde{H})\cap [0,E^+)=\left\{\frac{kE^+}{m}:k=0,....,m-1\right\},
\end{equation}
we have that
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi}=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\sqrt{p_k}\ket{\tilde{E}_k}+\sqrt{p_m}\ket{\psi^+},
\end{equation}
for some normalized states $\{\ket{\tilde{E}_k}\}_k$ with $\tilde{H}\ket{\tilde{E}_k}=\tilde{E}_k\ket{\tilde{E}_k}$, $\tilde{E}_k:=\frac{k E^+}{m}$. The normalized state $\ket{\psi^+}$ lives in the high energy sector of $\tilde{H}$, with energy support in $[E^+,\infty)$. Moreover, from eq. (\ref{discre_phi}) we infer that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}p_k\tilde{E}_k + p_mE^+\leq \bar{E}.
\end{equation}

Now, define the positive semidefinite matrices
\begin{equation}
\tilde{M}_{a|x}:=\Phi M_{a|x}\Phi^\dagger,
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
\Phi:=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\sqrt{p_k}\ket{k}\bra{E_k}+\sqrt{p_{m}}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\ket{j+m}\bra{\psi^+_j},
\end{equation}
and $\ket{\psi^+_j}:=e^{-i\tilde{H}t_j}\ket{\psi^+}$. 

Then it holds that
\begin{align}
p(a|x,j,\tilde{M})&:=\bra{\tilde{\psi}_j}\tilde{M}_{a|x}\ket{\tilde{\psi}_j}\nonumber\\
&=\bra{\psi}e^{i\tilde{H}t_j}M_{a|x}e^{-i\tilde{H}t_j}\ket{\psi}\nonumber\\
&=\tilde{P}(a|x,t_j),\forall j,a,x,
\label{tilde2POVM}
\end{align}
with
\begin{equation}
\ket{\tilde{\psi}_j}:=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}e^{-iE_kt_j}\ket{k}+\ket{m+j},
\end{equation}
for $j=1,...,N$. In addition, 
\begin{equation}
\sum_a\tilde{M}_{a|x}=\Phi\Phi^\dagger=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\mbox{diag}(p_1,...,p_{m-1})&0\\0&\gamma\end{array}\right),
\label{norm_cond_aux}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_{jk}:=p_{m}\braket{\psi^+_j}{\psi^+_k}$. The structure of the matrix $\gamma$ will occupy us in the next section, but, for the moment, it is enough to notice that $\gamma$ belongs to the following set of matrices:
\begin{defin}
\label{defin_C_N}
$C_N$ is the cone of all $N\times N$ complex, positive semidefinite matrices $\gamma$ satisfying $\gamma_{jj}=\gamma_{kk}$, for $j,k=1,...,N$.
\end{defin}

Putting all these properties together, we arrive at the following definition:
\begin{defin}
$A_m(\bar{E};E^+)$ is the set of all datasets $Q$ (with times $t_1,...,t_N$) such that there exist a normalized distribution $(p_j)_{j=0}^m$,
a matrix $\gamma\in C_N$ and $(m+N)\times (m+N)$ matrices $\{\tilde{M}_{a|x}\}_{a,x}$ satisfying:
\begin{align}
&\sum_{j=0}^mp_{j}\frac{E^+j}{m}\leq \bar{E},\\
\label{aver_spec}
&\tilde{M}_{a|x}\geq 0, \forall a,x,\\
&\sum_a\tilde{M}_{a|x}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\mbox{diag}(p_0,...,p_{m-1})&0\\0&\gamma\end{array}\right),\forall x,\\
&\gamma_{11}=p_m,\\
&\sum_a|Q(a|x,t_j)-f(a|x,j,\tilde{M})|\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+ |t_j|}{m}\right),\forall a,x,j.
\end{align}
\end{defin}


From what we have seen above, for 
\begin{equation}
m>\pi E^+\max_j|t_j|.
\label{rel_m_and_E_plus}
\end{equation}
it holds that $A_m(\bar{E};E^+)\supset A(\bar{E})$. Moreover, $A_m(\bar{E};E^+)$ is SDP representable. The next Lemma bounds the distance between $A_m(\bar{E};E^+)$ and $A(\bar{E})$. 
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma_speed_conv_A_hierarhy}
Let $Q\in A_m(\bar{E};E^+)$. Then, there exists $\check{P}\in A(\bar{E})$ such that
\begin{align}
&\sum_a|Q(a|x,t_j)-\check{P}(a|x,t_j)|\nonumber\\
&\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+ |t_j|}{m}\right)+2\sqrt{\frac{\bar{E}}{E^+}}.
\end{align}
In particular, setting $E^+=E^+(m):=\left(\frac{\bar{E}}{4\texttt{t}^2}\right)^{1/3}m^{2/3}$, with $\texttt{t}=\max_j|t_j|$, we have that 
\begin{equation}
\|Q(t_j)-\check{P}(t_j)\|\leq 2\left(\frac{1}{4^{1/3}}+2^{1/3}\right)\left(\frac{\bar{E}\texttt{t}}{m}\right)^{1/3}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
The reader can find the proof in Appendix \ref{app:proof_conv_A}. Define $A_m(\bar{E}):=A_m(\bar{E};E^+(m))$. Then, $A_m(\bar{E})$ is an SDP relaxation of $A(\bar{E})$. Moreover, by the lemma above, it holds that $\lim_{m\to\infty} A_m(\bar{E})=\overline{A(\bar{E})}$.


\subsection{Soft energy support constraint}
Note that, to properly pinpoint any of the sets of datasets $S(E^+)$, $S(E^+;\epsilon)$, $A(\bar{E})$, one needs to specify the vector $\vec{t}=(t_1,....,t_N)$ of measurement times. As expected, the parameters of the characterizations we found for $S(E^+)$, $A(\bar{E})$ depended continuously on the variables $\vec{t}$. This is not the case, however, with $S(E^+;\epsilon)$. For the time being, we will thus refer to it as $S(E^+;\epsilon, \vec{t})$, i.e., we will specify $\vec{t}$.

The characterization of $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is linked to that of the simpler set:
\begin{defin}[Weak support and spectral constraints]
For $\vec{E}=(E_1,...,E_m)$, with $E_k\leq E^+,\forall k$, we call $S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ the set of datasets $\{P(a|x,t_j)\}_j$ with quantum realization $(\H, \rho,H, M)$ such that $\mbox{spec}(H)\cap [0,E^+)=\{E_k\}_k$ and
\begin{equation}
\int_{E^+}^\infty\sigma(E)dE\leq \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(E)dE$ denotes the energy distribution of $\rho$.
\end{defin}
We will first derive a tractable characterization of this set.

Let $P\in S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$, with pure-state realization $(\H,\ket{\psi},H, M_{a|x})$. Then we can express $\ket{\psi}$ as
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi}=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1} \sqrt{p_k}\ket{E_k}+\sqrt{p_{m}}\ket{\psi^+},
\end{equation}
with $H\ket{E_k}=E_k\ket{E_k}$ and such that the support of the energy distribution of $\ket{\psi^+}$ is contained in $[E^+,\infty)$.

By the same argument from which we arrived at the definition of $A_m(\bar{E};E^+)$, we find that 
\begin{align}
\tilde{P}(a|x,t_j)=p(a|x,j,\tilde{M}),\forall j,a,x,
\end{align}
for some positive semidefinite matrices $\{\tilde{M}_{a|x}\}_{a,x}$ satisfying (\ref{norm_cond_aux}) for some matrix $\gamma$, with $\gamma_{jk}:=p_{m}\braket{\psi^+_j}{\psi^+_k}$. Note that the overlaps $\{\braket{\psi^+_j}{\psi^+_k}\}_{jk}$ are not arbitrary: by assumption, there must exist a non-normalized vector $\ket{\tilde{\psi}^+}$ in some Hilbert space $\tilde{H}$ and some operator $H^+$, with $H^+\geq E^+$, such that 
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{jk}=\bra{\tilde{\psi}^+}e^{-iH^+(t_k-t_j)}\ket{\tilde{\psi}^+},\forall j,k.
\label{hamil2gamma}
\end{equation}
This requirement motivates the following definition:
\begin{defin}
The set of \emph{decay matrices} $G(\vec{t})$ is the closure of the cone of square matrices $\gamma$ such that
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{jk}=\int_{-\infty}^\infty dE e^{-iE(t_k-t_j)}\rho^+(E),j,k=1,...,N,
\label{def_G}
\end{equation}
for some non-normalized measure $\rho^+(E)$. 
\end{defin}
In Appendix \ref{app:overlaps}, we prove that any decay matrix $\gamma\in G(\vec{t})$ can be arbitrarily well approximated by a matrix of the form (\ref{hamil2gamma}), with the integration region $[E^+,\infty)$.

We are ready to provide an alternative characterization of $S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon)$.
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma_high_energy}
$P\in \overline{S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})}$ iff there exist a distribution $\{p_j\}_j$, $N\times $ matrices $\{\tilde{M}_{a|x}\}_{a,x}$ and $\gamma\in G(\vec{t})$ such that
\begin{align}
&p_m\leq \epsilon,\label{cond_soft1}\\
&\tilde{M}_{a|x}\geq 0,\forall a,x,\label{cond_soft2}\\
&\sum_a\tilde{M}_{a|x}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}\mbox{diag}(p_1,...,p_{m-1})&0\\0&\gamma\end{array}\right),\label{cond_soft3}\\
&P(a|x,t_j)=p(a|x,j,\tilde{M}),\forall a,x,j.\label{cond_soft4}
\end{align}

\end{lemma}
See Appendix \ref{app:proof_high_energy} for a full proof. Curiously enough, the set of decay matrices $G(\vec{t})$ is not continuous in $\vec{t}$, see Appendix \ref{app:overlaps}. This suggests that the sets of datasets $S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ might also be discontinuous in the vector variable $\vec{t}$. In fact, as we show in Section \ref{sec:discontinuity}, even the more complicated set $S(E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is discontinuous in time.




Lemma \ref{lemma_high_energy} provides a characterization of $S(\vec{E},E^+,\epsilon)$ in terms of $G(\vec{t})$. In Appendix \ref{app:overlaps}, we show that, for some time vectors $\vec{t}$, the set $G(\vec{t})$ is SDP representable. This is, in fact, the case if, for some $\Delta>0$, it holds that $t_j=k_j\Delta$, for $j=1,...,N$, where $k_j\in\mathbb{N}$. Similarly, for $N=3$, if the real numbers $t_1-t_3,t_2- t_3$ are not congruent (namely, if no non-zero linear combination thereof with rational coefficients vanishes), then the set $G(\vec{t})$ coincides with the SDP representable set $C_3$, see Definition \ref{defin_C_N}. In these two cases, the conditions of Lemma \ref{lemma_high_energy} define an SDP to characterize $S(\vec{E},E^+,\epsilon)$.

In general, though, $G(\vec{t})$ needs to be characterized through hierarchies of SDP relaxations. Namely, there exist SDP representable sets $(G_k(\vec{t}))_{k\in\N}$ such that $G_1(\vec{t})\supset G_2(\vec{t})\supset...\supset G(\vec{t})$, with $\lim_{k\to\infty}G_k(\vec{t})=G_(\vec{t})$. Each set $G_k(\vec{t})$ corresponds to the Lasserre-Parrilo SDP relaxation of order $k$ \cite{lasserre, parrilo} of the set of measures on complex variables subject to certain polynomial constraints, see Appendix \ref{app:overlaps} for details. 

If the real numbers $\{t_k-t_N\}_{k=1}^{N-1}$ are non-congruent, then the resulting set of decay matrices is known in the literature on convex optimization as $\mbox{CUT}^\infty_N$ \cite{lifting2} (note that the set of decay matrices does not depend on the exact values of $t_1,...,t_N$, as long as the non-congruency condition holds). In \cite{lifting} it is proven that $\mbox{CUT}^\infty_N\subsetneq C_N$, for all $N\geq 4$; in particular, $\mbox{CUT}^\infty_N$ satisfies a number of linear inequalities, violated by the larger set $C_N$. It is easy to see that $\mbox{CUT}^\infty_N\supset G(\vec{s})$, for any vector of measurement times $\vec{s}\in \R^N$, see Appendix \ref{app:overlaps}.


Given the relaxations $(G_k(\vec{t}))_k$, one just needs to replace $G$ by $G_k$ in Lemma \ref{lemma_high_energy} to arrive at an SDP relaxation $S^k(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ of $S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$. Moreover, $\lim_{k\to\infty}S^k(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})=\overline{S(\vec{E};E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})}$.

To arrive at SDP relaxations for $S(E^+;\epsilon, \vec{t})$, we use again the discretization function (\ref{def_floor_disc}). Let $P\in S(E^+;\epsilon, \vec{t})$, with realization $(\H,\rho,H,M)$. Then we have that the energy distribution $\tilde{\sigma}(E)dE$ of $\rho$, with respect to the Hamiltonian $\tilde{H}:=\underline{\varphi}_m(H,E^+)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\int_{E^+}^{\infty} \tilde{\sigma}(E)dE\leq \epsilon.
\end{equation}
Thus, the timeline
\begin{equation}
\tilde{P}(a|x,t):=\tr(e^{-i\tilde{H}t}\rho e^{i\tilde{H}t} M_{a|x})
\end{equation}
belongs to $S(\{E_k\}_{k=0}^{m-1};\epsilon,\vec{t})$, with $E_k=\frac{kE^+}{m}$. Which leads us to the following result:
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:hard2weak}
Define ${\cal E}_m:=\{0,\frac{E^+}{m},...,\frac{(m-1)E^+}{m}\}$ and let $P\in S(E^+,\epsilon)$. Then, for any $m\in \N$, $m>\pi E^+\max_j|t_j|$, there exists $\tilde{P}\in S({\cal E}_m;E^+,\epsilon)$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|P(t_j)-\tilde{P}(t_j)\|\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+|t_j|}{m}\right),\forall x.
\label{approx_by_disc}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

Depending on $\vec{t}$, $S({\cal E}_m;E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ will be characterizable through an SDP or an SDP hierarchy $(S^n({\cal E}_m;E^+,\epsilon))_n$. In either case, this suggests a simple hierarchy of relaxations for $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$.
\begin{defin}
Let $\vec{t}$ be such that $G(\vec{t})$ is SDP representable and let $m\in\N$, with $m>\pi E^+\max_j|t_j|$. Then $S_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is the set of datasets $P$ such that there exists $\tilde{P}\in S({\cal E}_m;E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ with
    \begin{equation}
    \|P(t_j)-\tilde{P}(t_j)\|\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+|t_j|}{m}\right),\forall x,j.
    \end{equation}
\end{defin}
\begin{defin}
Let $\vec{t}$ be such that $G(\vec{t})$ admits a characterization in terms of the SDP hierarchy $(G^k(\vec{t}))_k$ and let $m\in\N$, with $m>\pi E^+\max_j|t_j|$. Then $S^k_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is the set of datasets $P$ such that there exists $\tilde{P}\in S^k({\cal E}_m;E^+,\epsilon,\vec{t})$ with
    \begin{equation}
    \|P(t_j)-\tilde{P}(t_j)\|\leq 2\sin\left(\frac{E^+|t_j|}{m}\right),\forall x,j.
    \end{equation}

\end{defin}
From the two definitions, it is clear that $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is a subset of either $S_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ or $S_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$. Moreover, the two last sets are SDP representable. 

How accurately do $S_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$, $S^k_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ approximate $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$? By definition, any element of $S_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is $O(1/m)$-away from an element of the inner set $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$. Thanks to recent results in optimization theory \cite{bounds_lasserre}, we were able to estimate how far an element in $S^k_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ can be from the target set $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop_asymptotic}
Let $P\in S^k_m(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$. Then, there exists $\hat{P}\in  S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|\check{P}(t_j)-P(t_j)\|\leq O\left(\frac{N^2}{k^2}\right)+O\left(\frac{E^+\texttt{t}}{m}\right),\forall j,
\end{equation}
with $\texttt{t}:=\max_{j}|t_j|$.
\end{prop}
For exact upper bounds and a proof of the result, please check Appendix \ref{app:proof_asymptotic}.






\subsubsection{$S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is discontinuous in $\vec{t}$}
\label{sec:discontinuity}
Let $E^+, \Delta> 0$, and consider the following dataset, defined in the $X=1,A=2$ timed measurement scenario:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{P}(0|t_1)=1,\tilde{P}(0|t_2)=0,
\end{equation}
with $t_1=0,t_2=\Delta$. Let $\delta(j)=\delta>0$ and, for $m\in \N$, define $\tau_m:=2\Delta+\frac{\Delta}{2m+1}$. We wish to find the minimum value of $P(0|\tau_m)$, for all $P\in S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2}\right)$ that fit the noisy dataset $(\tilde{P},\delta)$. Namely, we wish to solve the optimization problem:
\begin{align}
p_m:=&\min_{P}P(0|\tau_m)\nonumber\\
\mbox{such that }&P \mbox{ fits } (\tilde{P},\delta),\nonumber\\
&P\in S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2}\right).
\end{align}

It is easy to see that, for all $m\in\N$, $p_m=0$. Indeed, consider the realization $(\C^2,\proj{+}, H_m, M)$, with
\begin{align}
&\ket{+}:=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{0}+\ket{1}),\nonumber\\
&H_m:=\frac{(2m+1)\pi}{\Delta}\proj{1},\nonumber\\
&M_0=\proj{+},M_1=\id-\proj{+}.
\end{align}
It can be verified that the corresponding timeline $P_m\in S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2}\right)$ fits $\tilde{P}$ exactly and predicts $P_m(0|\tau_m)=0$.

Note that $\tau:=\lim_{m\to\infty}\tau_m=2\Delta$. If $S(E^+;\frac{1}{2},\vec{t})$ were continuous in $\vec{t}$, we would conclude that the solution of the problem
\begin{align}
p=&\min_{P}P(0|\tau)\nonumber\\
\mbox{such that }&P \mbox{ fits } (\tilde{P},\delta),\nonumber\\
&P\in S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2}\right)
\end{align}
would satisfy $p=\lim_{m\to\infty}p_m=0$. 

However, this is not the case. In Appendix \ref{app:discontinuity_soft}, we prove the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:discontinuity_soft}
Let $\Delta \leq \frac{\pi}{2E^+}$, and let $P\in S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2}\right)$ fit $(\tilde{P},\delta)$. Then, 
\begin{equation}
1-P(0|\tau)\leq \sqrt{O(\sqrt{\delta})+O((E^+\Delta)^2)}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
That is, for small enough $\Delta$ and $\delta$, $P(0|\tau)$ must be very close to $1$.

The set of datasets $S\left(E^+;\frac{1}{2},\vec{t}\right)$ does not depend continuously on the measurement time $\vec{t}$, how can that be? Well, conducting a measurement at a specific time $t$ is an idealization: the closest one can do in practice is conducting a measurement at time $t$ \emph{approximately}. Given the realization $(\C^2,\proj{+}, H_m, M)$, let us recompute the observed measurement statistics assuming a Gaussian distribution of the measurement time $t$ of width $\epsilon$ and mean $\bar{t}$. We find that the resulting averaged timeline $\bar{P}_m$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\bar{P}_m(0|\bar{t})=\frac{1}{2}\left[1+e^{-(2m+1)^2\pi^2\epsilon^2}\cos\left(\frac{(2m+1)\pi}{\Delta}\right)\right].
\end{equation}
For $\epsilon\not=0$, the absolute value of the differential $\frac{d}{d\bar{t}}\bar{P}_m(0|\bar{t})$ is of order $me^{-O(m^2)}$, which is bounded. This evidences that the set $S(E^+;\epsilon)$ becomes continuous under the averaging of the measurement times. We believe that such ``averaged sets'' can be characterized using tools similar to the ones developed in this section. Such a characterization, though, goes beyond the scope of this work.

In view of the above, the `exact' set of datasets $S(E^+;\epsilon,\vec{t})$ is still useful to limit the measurement statistics one can generate with a \emph{specific} equipment (namely one with a given, albeit unknown realization) in the limit where the precision on the measurement times becomes infinite. However, in information processing scenarios where an adversary might exploit the lack of control over the measurement times, such as in randomness certification \cite{jones2025certifiedrandomnessquantumspeed}, one would have to assume the set of decay matrices to be as much general as possible. Namely, one should model decay matrices using the set $\mbox{CUT}^{\infty}_N$ instead of $G(t)$ in the corresponding SDP relaxation.





\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusions}

In this paper, we have introduced the problem of extrapolation of quantum time series under energy constraints. While high-precision experimental data is generally required to make non-trivial predictions, we found that fairly simple datasets allow long-time extrapolations under reasonable experimental precision. Such datasets are, moreover, self-testing: their approximate realization allows one to approximately pinpoint their underlying quantum realization. We also identified two unexpected extrapolation phenomena: aha! datasets and fog banks. In addition, we showed that the extrapolation problem can be efficiently solved for different energy constraints through complete hierarchies of SDP relaxations. 

For future research, it would be interesting to explore time-evolving scenarios with non-demolition measurements. A natural goal in that framework would be predicting the next measurement outcome, given a string of past results. Another research line worth investigating is whether the characterization of quantum time series could be of any use in the debate on the nature of gravity \cite{BMM+17, MV17}. If gravity is a quantum field \cite{Rovelli_2004, Becker_Becker_Schwarz_2006}, then any experimental time series generated during the gravitational interaction of two or more quantum particles is subject to the constraints that we describe in this paper. On the contrary, if gravity is classical \cite{TD16, oppenheim}, one such experiment could generate time series impossible to account for, under both the quantum hypothesis and some assumption on the energy of the interaction. Could this train of thought lead to an experimental disproof of quantum gravity?


