In SFI, agents are guided to influence selected CoG state dimensions using an intrinsic reward $\sum_{d\in \mathrm{CoG}\delta} \mathrm{Inf}_t^d$ that promotes interaction with low-entropy components. In contrast, AFI applies agent-level focusing across all state dimensions without CoG selection, where the intrinsic reward is given by $\sum_{d=0}^{D-1} \mathrm{Inf}_t^d \cdot \text{clip}(e_{t-1}^d, 1, c_{\max})$. FIM combines both selective targeting and synchronized persistence via the intrinsic reward structure in Eq.\eqref{eq:intrew}. We observe that only FIM consistently succeeds in solving the task. Vanilla QMIX alone fails due to ineffective exploration. SFI enhances interaction with critical states, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:pushbox}(c), but struggles to maintain consistent focus on a single target, as seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:pushbox-dist-change}, which leads to task failure. AFI promotes sustained influence when combined with SFI, yet fails on its own due to the absence of targeted attention. These results emphasize that both principled state selection and agent-level coordination are essential for effective cooperation in sparse-rewarded environments.

\subsection{Performance Comparison on Complex MARL Benchmarks: SMAC and GRF}
