\section{Implementation Details}

\subsection{Empirical Implementation of State Focusing Influence}
\label{subsecapp:sfi-imp-detail}

To estimate $\mathcal{H}(d)$, we approximate the marginal distribution of normalized changes $p\left(\Delta^d(s_t, s_{t+1})\right)$, since conditioning on full states $p\left(\Delta^d(s_t, s_{t+1}) \mid s_t\right)$ is computationally prohibitive. We construct the empirical distribution $\hat{p}\left(\Delta^d(s_t, s_{t+1})\right)$ by counting occurrences discretized to two decimal places from 100K episodes collected under a random policy. The entropy is then computed as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}(d) \approx \mathbb{E}_\rho \left[ -\log \hat{p}(\Delta^d(s_t, s_{t+1})) \right]
\end{equation}
To ensure comparability across environments, $\mathcal{H}(d)$ values for $d \in \mathrm{CoG}_\delta$ are min-max normalized to the range $[0, 1]$ within each environment.

The counterfactual intrinsic reward in Eq.~\ref{eq:statefocus} is computed as the sum of $\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(\cdot)$ over agents $i$, where $\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(\cdot)$ measures the influence of agent $i$ on state dimension $s^d$ at time $t$:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1}) = \left| \hat{s}_{t+1}^d(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) - s^d_t \right| - \mathbb{E}_{a^i_t \sim \rho^i} \left[ \left| \hat{s}_{t+1}^d(s_t, a^i_t, \mathbf{a}^{-i}_t) - s^d_t \right| \right]
\end{equation}

In practice, the expectation over counterfactual actions $a^i_t \sim \rho^i$ is approximated by uniformly averaging over the set of actions $\mathcal{A}^i_t$ available to agent $i$ at time $t$, yielding:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1}) \approx \left| \hat{s}_{t+1}^d(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) - s^d_t \right| - \frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}^i_t|} \sum_{a^i_t \in \mathcal{A}^i_t} \left| \hat{s}_{t+1}^d(s_t, a^i_t, \mathbf{a}^{-i}_t) - s^d_t \right|
\end{equation}

The transition model $\hat{s}$ used to compute $\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(\cdot)$ is implemented as a three-layer multilayer perceptron (MLP) and trained by minimizing the following mean squared error loss:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\hat{s}} = \mathbb{E}_{s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1}} \left[ \left\| \hat{s}(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) - s_{t+1} \right\|^2 \right]
\end{equation}

Since the influence is estimated using a learned model, approximation noise can introduce spurious nonzero signals even when agent $i$ has no actual effect on $s^d$. To mitigate false positives, we discard any $\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(\cdot)$ below a threshold $\kappa$, and mask out agents that are inactive or dead at time $t$. The final influence on dimension $s^d$ is computed by summing only the valid contributions:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Inf}^d_t(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1}) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}_t} \mathbf{1}[\mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1}) \geq \kappa] \cdot \mathrm{Inf}^{d,i}_t(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, s_{t+1})
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{N}_t$ denotes the set of active agents at time $t$, and $\mathbf{1}[\cdot]$ is the indicator function.


\subsection{Complete Implementation and Algorithmic Details of FIM}
\label{subsecapp:full-imp-detail}
The FIM framework builds on the centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) paradigm, using QMIX to learn a joint action-value function. Each agent maintains an individual $Q$-function $Q^i(\tau^i_t, a^i_t)$ based on its action-observation history $\tau^i_t$ and current action $a^i_t$. These per-agent utilities are combined via a mixing network to produce a global joint $Q$-value, $Q_\theta^{\mathrm{tot}}(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t)$, where $\theta$ denotes the parameters of the mixing network.

To stabilize learning, FIM employs a target mixing network $Q_{\theta^-}^{\mathrm{tot}}$, which is periodically updated by overwriting its parameters with those of the current mixing network. The temporal difference (TD) loss is computed using a Bellman update that incorporates both extrinsic and intrinsic rewards:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TD}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{s, \mathbf{a}, r, s'} \left[ \left( r_{\mathrm{ext},t} + \alpha r_{\mathrm{int},t} + \gamma \max_{\mathbf{a}'} Q_{\theta^-}^{\mathrm{tot}}(s_{t+1}, \mathbf{a}') - Q_\theta^{\mathrm{tot}}(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) \right)^2 \right]
\end{equation}
This loss is minimized using the Adam optimizer to update the parameters $\theta$, while the target network parameters $\theta^-$ are synchronized at fixed intervals. The complete training procedure of FIM is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:FIM}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\DontPrintSemicolon
\SetArgSty{textnormal}
\SetKwInput{Init}{Initialize}
\caption{FIM framework}
\label{alg:FIM}
\Init{Q network, dynamics model $\hat{s}$}
Collect random trajectories under random behavior\;
Approximate $\mathcal{H}(d)$ with the random trajectories based on Eq.~(4)\;
Define CoG state dimensions $\mathrm{CoG}_\delta$ based on Eq.~(5)\;
Compute $w_d$ for each $d \in \mathrm{CoG}_\delta$\;
\For{training iteration}{
    \For{timestep $t$}{
        Sample transition $(s_t,\mathbf{o}_t,\mathbf{a}_t,s_{t+1}, \mathbf{o}_{t+1})$ using $\boldsymbol{\pi}$, where $\mathbf{o}_t=(o^0_t,\cdots,o^{n-1}_t)$ \;
        \For{$d \in \mathrm{CoG}_\delta$}{
            Compute collective influence $\mathrm{Inf}^d_t$ by Eq.~(6)\;
            Update eligibility trace $e^d_t$ by Eq.~(7)\;          
        }
        Compute intrinsic reward $r_{\mathrm{int},t}$ by Eq.~(8)\;
    }
    Update value function $Q^{\mathrm{tot}}$ and dynamics model $\hat{s}$\;
}
\end{algorithm}


\section{Environment Details}
\label{secapp:env-details}
\textbf{Push-2-Box}

Push-2-Box is a cooperative multi-agent environment where two agents must jointly push one of two boxes toward a wall to obtain a reward. A box moves one cell if pushed by a single agent and two cells if pushed simultaneously. Thus, synchronized cooperation is essential for completing the task within the episode time limit. The environment terminates either when a box reaches the wall or when the episode length is exceeded.

%Push-2-Box는 두 명의 에이전트가 협력하여 두 개의 박스 중 하나를 벽까지 밀어 보상을 얻는 협력형 다중 에이전트 환경입니다. 각 에이전트는 독립적으로 이동하고 인접한 박스에 힘을 가할 수 있으며, 한 명의 에이전트가 밀면 박스는 한 칸 이동하고, 두 명이 동시에 밀면 두 칸 이동합니다. 따라서 주어진 에피소드 시간 내에 작업을 완료하기 위해서는 동기화된 협력이 필수적입니다. 환경은 박스가 벽에 도달하거나 에피소드 제한 시간이 초과될 경우 종료됩니다.

The \textbf{state space} consists of the $(x, y)$ positions of both agents and both boxes, resulting in an 8-dimensional state vector. To isolate coordination from partial observability, each agent receives the full environment state as observation. The \textbf{action space} is discrete, consisting of eight movement actions corresponding to up, down, left, right, top-right, right-down, down-left, and left-top directions. The \textbf{reward function} is sparse, providing +100 if a box reaches the wall and -1 if no box reaches the wall by the end of the episode.

%\textbf{상태 공간(state space)}은 두 에이전트와 두 박스의 $(x, y)$ 좌표로 구성되어 총 8차원의 상태 벡터를 이룹니다. 부분 관찰로 인한 난이도 요소를 제거하고 순수한 협력 능력을 평가하기 위해, 모든 에이전트가 매 시점 전체 환경 상태를 동일하게 관찰할 수 있도록 설정을 완화하였습니다. \textbf{행동 공간(action space)}은 상, 하, 좌, 우, 우상단, 우하단, 좌하단, 좌상단 방향 이동으로 구성된 8개의 이산형 액션으로 구성됩니다. \textbf{보상 함수(reward function)}는 희소(sparse) 설정으로, 박스가 벽에 도달하면 +100의 보상을, 에피소드 종료 시까지 어떤 박스도 벽에 도달하지 못할 경우 -1의 패널티를 제공합니다.


\textbf{StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge (SMAC)}

SMAC~\cite{samvelyan2019starcraft} is a benchmark for evaluating decentralized cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning. Agents control individual StarCraft II units and must coordinate to defeat enemy forces operated by a scripted AI. During centralized training, a global state is accessible, but during decentralized execution, each agent relies solely on its local observations within a limited sight range. SMAC offers both dense and sparse reward settings, with the sparse reward setting significantly increasing the difficulty by removing intermediate feedback.

%StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge (SMAC)~\cite{samvelyan2019starcraft}는 분산 협력형 다중 에이전트 강화 학습을 평가하기 위한 벤치마크입니다. 에이전트들은 StarCraft II 유닛을 개별적으로 제어하며, 스크립트된 AI가 조작하는 적 유닛을 물리치기 위해 협력해야 합니다. 중앙집중형 훈련(centralized training) 동안에는 전역 상태(global state)에 접근할 수 있지만, 실행(execution) 시에는 각 에이전트가 제한된 시야 범위 내의 지역 관찰(local observation)만을 기반으로 행동합니다. SMAC은 밀집(dense) 보상과 희소(sparse) 보상 설정을 모두 제공하며, 본 논문에서는 중간 피드백이 제거된 희소 보상 설정을 사용하여 학습 난이도를 높였습니다.


The \textbf{state space} aggregates absolute features of all units, including positions, health, shields, energies, cooldowns, unit types, and past actions. The \textbf{observation space} provides each agent with relative $(x, y)$ positions, health, shield status, and unit types of nearby allies and enemies. The \textbf{action space} is discrete, consisting of movement in four directions, attacking visible enemies, stopping, and no-op actions (only allowed for dead units). The \textbf{reward function} is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:smac-reward}, and our experiments focus on the sparse reward setting across eight challenging scenarios. Scenario visualizations are provided in Fig.~\ref{fig:smac-render}, with unit compositions and environment dimensions summarized in Table~\ref{tab:smac-conf}.


%\textbf{상태 공간}은 모든 유닛의 절대적 특징(위치, 체력, 보호막, 에너지, 쿨다운, 유닛 타입, 과거 행동 등)을 집계한 정보를 포함합니다. \textbf{관찰 공간(observation space)}은 인접한 아군 및 적군 유닛들의 상대적 $(x, y)$ 위치, 체력, 보호막 상태, 유닛 타입 등의 정보를 제공합니다. \textbf{행동 공간}은 네 방향 이동, 관찰 가능한 적 유닛 공격, 정지(stop), 그리고 죽은 유닛에만 허용되는 무행동(no-op)으로 구성된 이산형 공간입니다. \textbf{보상 함수}는 Table~\ref{tab:smac-reward}에 요약되어 있으며, 본 연구에서는 희소 보상 설정을 사용하여 총 여덟 개의 난이도 높은 시나리오를 실험 대상으로 삼았습니다.

