The region of enhancement between $3.3$~\edit{\AA$^{-1}$}$<Q<4.3$~\AA$^{-1}$ also exhibits distinct signatures of the valence electron dynamics. In the experimental data, the enhancement initially rises, then begins to fall again after 100~fs. This behavior is qualitatively reproduced by the \textit{ab initio} theory, but not by the IAM signal that shows a monotonic rise. The qualitative discrepancy between the IAM signal and the experimental data demonstrates that the dominant effect we observe here is the dynamics of the \edit{photoexcited} valence electron rather than the structural dynamics of the deuteron dissociation. 

